00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.500
So now back to the agenda.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.810
What is Firebase?

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.070
Firebase is a platform for
building extraordinary apps.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:34.470
What does that mean?

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:38.740
Firebase consists of
three core features,

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.070
and the first of those
is a real-time database.

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.270
I'm going to talk about
all these in detail

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:46.790
but the Firebase database
is a NoSQL, JSON database.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:48.800
And every piece of
data stored in Firebase

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.370
is referenced by a URL.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.070
The second feature of Firebase
is user authentication.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.419
So how many of you have
implemented OAuth before?

00:02:57.419 --> 00:02:57.960
A few of you.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.470
How many of you enjoyed it?

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:01.220
One person.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:01.720
One person.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:02.916
It's not very fun.

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:04.290
But the cool thing
about Firebase

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:07.110
is Firebase makes user
authentication easy.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.970
So we take care of OAuth
on the Firebase servers.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:13.200
All you have to do
is one method call,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.390
different on each platform, and
you've got users authenticated.

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:18.992
You'll see that in demo shortly.

00:03:18.992 --> 00:03:20.700
And then the last
feature Firebase offers

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.130
is static hosting.

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:24.220
So this is a tool
for web developers

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.442
to deploy static content,
HTML, CSS, and JavaScript

00:03:27.442 --> 00:03:29.400
through the command line
with just one command,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.690
Firebase deploy.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:35.970
And your app is live on your
own Firebase app .com domain.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:40.710
So let's take a look at
the real-time database.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:43.560
First, I want to talk about
the ability of Firebase

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.534
to allow devices to talk
directly to the database.

00:03:46.534 --> 00:03:48.700
So what we have here is
your traditional application

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:49.770
architecture.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.110
We've got a device that talks to
an application server, which is

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:54.630
running your application logic.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.470
And then this in turn
talks to the database.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.250
With Firebase the
architecture is going

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.420
to look a little different.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.730
With Firebase we have a
two-tier architecture.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:06.300
So your device can talk directly
to the Firebase database.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.260
And this simplifies
development a lot.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.267
With this two-tier architecture
development is much faster.

00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:14.600
So typically building real-time
apps is something that's

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:16.899
pretty difficult. We have
things like Google Docs

00:04:16.899 --> 00:04:19.920
where users are adding notes
to documents in real time.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.490
There's a team of hundreds
of engineers behind that.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:24.850
With Firebase you can
build real-time app

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.350
as just a single developer or
a small team of developers.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.930
So development is really fast.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:32.209
And then Firebase handles
all the hard parts for you

00:04:32.209 --> 00:04:34.250
so you don't have to worry
about scaling your app

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.226
from your first 10 users
to your first 10,000

00:04:37.226 --> 00:04:38.350
if your app is a smash hit.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:42.480
Firebase will take care of
the scaling part for you.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.330
But sometimes you will want
to run trusted server code.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.450
A lot of the Firebase
customers do.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:50.210
So Firebase has a REST API that
lets you connect your server

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:51.550
code to Firebase.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:54.900
And it's also really easy
to wire up third party APIs

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:58.604
with Firebase if
you'd like to do that.

00:04:58.604 --> 00:05:00.020
So my favorite
part about Firebase

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.110
is the real-time data
synchronization, which you

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.142
can see from this awesome gif.

00:05:04.142 --> 00:05:05.600
So the cool thing
about Firebase is

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.960
that any client that is
connected to your app

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:10.754
shares one master
copy of the data.

00:05:10.754 --> 00:05:12.170
Any client that
subscribes to that

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:13.910
will see data
updates in real time.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:16.130
So you don't have to pull
to refresh on mobile,

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.550
you don't have to refresh
the page on the web.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:23.589
Data is synchronized in real
time, which is really cool.

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:25.130
So I've talked a
little bit about it.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:27.480
Let's look at some Swift
code on how you would store

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.570
and sync data in Firebase.

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:32.000
So as I mentioned before,
every piece of data in Firebase

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.810
has its own URL.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.580
So to create a reference
to your database

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:38.470
you say, let ref, so it's
going to be a constant here,

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:39.440
of type Firebase.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:40.960
And you pass it a URL.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.390
This is the URL I'm going
to use for this demo.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:45.650
And there's two ways to
write data to Firebase.

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:48.370
The first is set value in swift.

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.710
And what this will do is
it'll update the location

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:51.860
with whatever you pass it.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:55.870
You can pass it a string, an
object, a number, a Boolean,

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:57.500
and it'll update that.

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:00.490
So set value is useful
in certain situations.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:02.097
Sometimes you'll
have a lot of people

00:06:02.097 --> 00:06:04.180
writing to the same path
in your Firebase database

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:05.520
at the same time.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.530
And in this case, you'll want to
generate a unique ID each time

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.270
a new piece of data is added.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:13.030
Which is where you want to use
this method child by auto ID

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:14.352
in combination with set value.

00:06:14.352 --> 00:06:16.060
So here what we're
doing is we're saying,

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:18.530
ref dot child by
auto ID, which is

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:20.800
going to create a path at
the root of your Firebase,

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.650
slash a unique
ID, which is going

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:25.304
to look something like what
you see in the comment there.

00:06:25.304 --> 00:06:27.470
And then you're going to
set the value at that path.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:31.110
Here we're just setting it
to a string, "Hello again!".

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.150
So that's how you
write data to Firebase.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.220
To read data back
is pretty simple.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.300
You just attach this
asynchronous callback

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.000
to your Firebase reference.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.760
And this will be triggered any
time the data at that location

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:42.810
changes.

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:45.010
So you pass this
listener and event type.

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.320
Here we're using the
value event type.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:49.640
This function is going to
be called any time the value

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:51.780
at that Firebase
location changes.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.780
And because Firebase is
a real-time data stream,

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:57.020
it's always changing, this
will be passed as snapshot.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:59.910
So it's a snapshot of your data
at that specific point in time.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:02.160
And here we're just printing
the value of the snapshot

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.010
to the console.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:07.550
So now let's take a look at
Firebase's authentication

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:09.650
feature.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:12.660
Firebase has three different
types of authentication.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:14.974
You can do basic
e-mail password auth,

00:07:14.974 --> 00:07:16.640
and you can also
integrate with a number

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:17.770
of third-party providers.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.830
So if you'd like to add Google,
Facebook, Twitter, or GitHub

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:22.280
Auth to your app.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:24.030
It's really easy to
do this with Firebase.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:26.150
All you need to do is
register your application

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:29.700
with one of these services,
grab your API key in secret,

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:31.890
head on over to your
Firebase dashboard,

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:34.760
paste your API key in secret in
there, and then just one method

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:35.260
call.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:37.800
You're able to authenticate
users really easily.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.350
And then finally, Firebase
offers anonymous auth.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:43.020
Which is great if
you want to require

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:45.869
user login without having
users type in credentials.

00:07:45.869 --> 00:07:47.410
So if you want to
generate guest user

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:50.070
accounts and still
have some notion

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:54.530
of securing your data without
requiring users to log in.

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:56.840
Let's take a look
at some code here.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:59.440
This example we're using
GitHub authentication.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.300
So we just call the message
auth with OAuth provider.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:05.580
We pass it the provider
we want to use,

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.260
in this case it's GitHub.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:09.184
We'll pass it our access token.

00:08:09.184 --> 00:08:11.850
And then this is going to return
either an error or an auth data

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:12.730
object.

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:14.760
The auth data will
contain a bunch of data

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.550
from the GitHub user's profile.

00:08:19.276 --> 00:08:20.650
And so you're
probably wondering,

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:23.330
if I'm talking from the device
directly to the database,

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:24.440
how do I secure my data?

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.450
How do I make sure
that people can't

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:28.360
write to other user's nodes?

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.820
And the way you do this
is through security rules.

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:33.140
So Firebase has a powerful
security language.

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:36.679
All of the security rules are
run on the Firebase servers,

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:38.970
and they're written in
JSON-like syntex that's

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:42.330
going to match to the structure
of your data in Firebase.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:45.970
So let's say you have a slash
user's node in your Firebase.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:49.340
That dollar variable dollar
UID signifies a dynamic path.

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:52.810
So this rule is going to apply
to any child of slash users.

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:54.910
There's three types of
security rules in Firebase.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:56.830
The first is a read rule.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:59.070
So this is saying that
the data at slash users

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:01.010
slash UID is globally readable.

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:02.900
Anyone can read
the data, but it's

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:05.060
only writable by
that specific user.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:07.070
So when you use
Firebase authentication

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:08.860
in combination
with security rules

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:11.060
you automatically get access
to this Auth variable.

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:14.580
So I can do things like say,
only this specific user can

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:16.120
write to their own user node.

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:18.120
So this makes it possible
to say that you can't

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.180
spoof other users in the app.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:22.920
The third type of rule
is a validation rule.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:25.120
Those are run after the
right rule succeeds.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.800
You can do things like verify
that the new data is a string,

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:30.130
is a number, adheres
to a certain length,

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:33.106
and things like that.

00:09:33.106 --> 00:09:35.480
So a common problem on mobile
is what if your application

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:37.520
goes offline?

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:39.540
So let's say you got a
chat app that you built

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:42.910
and your users are on a train
that goes through a tunnel,

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:45.090
they lose connectivity,
they're climbing a mountain,

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:46.390
they're on a plane,
wherever they

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:48.530
may be they're going to
expect that their app will

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:50.400
work offline.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:53.460
So how does Firebase
handle offline situations?

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:55.490
So any data center
while offline is

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.460
persisted to disk in
Firebase, not just to memory,

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.200
which is really powerful.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:03.672
And when you regain
connectivity all of the data

00:10:03.672 --> 00:10:06.130
you sent while offline will be
sent to the Firebase servers

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:08.000
and you'll also pull
down any data that was

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.700
sent while you were offline.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:13.090
So I can talk about
offline disk persistence

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:16.790
but it's better
demonstrated with a demo.

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:19.290
And I didn't want to turn Wi-Fi
off during the presentation,

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:21.860
so I prerecorded this one.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:23.570
So what you're
going to see here is

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:26.050
we'll add one message
while we're online.

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:28.387
Then I'll turn the Wi-Fi
off, add another message,

00:10:28.387 --> 00:10:30.970
and then you'll see what happens
when I turn it back on again.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:33.440
So let's go ahead
and play this video.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.880
So you can see I'm online right
now adding a chat message.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:39.790
It was added to Firebase.

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:45.440
And now I'm turning my Wi-Fi
off, adding another message.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:49.630
Here we go, "Hello
from the other side".

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:51.390
And it was added to my app.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:52.650
I quit the app.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:54.960
Wasn't added to Firebase yet
because I'm still offline.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:57.190
You can see it's
still saved there.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:01.530
I'm turning my Wi-Fi back on and
boom, it was added to Firebase.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.460
So that's a quick demo of
how Firebase offline works.

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:11.151
And to enable this, you
just need one line of code.

00:11:11.151 --> 00:11:12.650
This is what it
looks like in Swift.

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:15.610
So you just say Firebase
dot default config and set

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:17.180
persistence enabled to true.

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:19.210
That's all you need to
do to get that working.

00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:20.970
And then optionally
you can specify

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:23.350
that data at a specific
location in your Firebase

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:25.820
should be pre-fetched
and kept up to date.

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:27.840
And you can do that with
reft dot keep synced

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:28.928
and set that to true.

00:11:31.104 --> 00:11:33.520
So now I want to talk about
the third feature of Firebase,

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:36.880
which is the static
hosting service.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:38.800
And this lets you deploy
your static assets

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:40.800
through the command
line with just one

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.330
command, Firebase deploy.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:45.540
And all the content is
served over HTTPS by default.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:48.980
Firebase takes care of
acquiring the SSL cert for you.

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:52.460
It's backed by a global CDN
and custom domain support

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:53.169
is available.

00:11:53.169 --> 00:11:55.710
So by default you get access to
your own Firebase app dot com

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:56.900
domain.

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:58.930
You can easily configure
a custom domain

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:02.997
if you'd like to host your
personal site on Firebase.

00:12:02.997 --> 00:12:05.580
It's really easy to do this once
you've installed the Firebase

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:06.930
command line tools.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:08.450
You just need to
run Firebase in it.

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:09.810
This will initialize
your directory

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:10.726
with Firebase hosting.

00:12:10.726 --> 00:12:13.770
You only ever have to run that
command once for each project.

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:16.270
And then after that to
deploy is just one command,

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:17.242
Firebase deploy.

00:12:17.242 --> 00:12:17.950
It's really fast.

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:24.090
So just to summarize, Firebase
has three client libraries.

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:26.070
iOS and Android
libraries for mobile.

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:29.110
I'm going to be focusing
on the iOS client today.

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:30.750
And then a JavaScript
client if you'd

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:32.669
like to build web
applications with Firebase.

00:12:32.669 --> 00:12:34.210
And if you're a
JavaScript developer,

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:35.820
Firebase has libraries
for integrating

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:38.680
with popular front end
frameworks like Ember, Angular,

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:40.140
Backbone, and React.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:42.464
So you can use those
frameworks for the front end

00:12:42.464 --> 00:12:43.880
of your application
and then store

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:45.750
all of your data in Firebase.

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:48.120
And then Firebase
also has a REST API

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:52.610
so you can access it from your
favorite server side language.

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:54.110
So now I'd like to
talk specifically

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:56.380
about how you can get
started with Firebase on iOS.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:12:57.890
Any iOS developers in the room?

00:12:57.890 --> 00:12:58.810
Just out of curiosity.

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:02.490
OK, a handful.

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.377
The easiest way to use Firebase
on iOS is through CocoaPods.

00:13:05.377 --> 00:13:06.960
For those of you
that aren't familiar,

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.740
CocoaPods is the iOS
dependency manager.

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:12.180
And so the way you do
this is you add Firebase

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:12.906
to your pod file.

00:13:12.906 --> 00:13:15.030
This is where you describe
all of your dependencies

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:18.030
for your application,
with the correct version.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:19.780
Then you go to your
application directory,

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:21.840
just run the
command pod install.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:23.410
That will install
all the pods you've

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:25.110
defined in your pod file.

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:27.350
And then be sure to open
the xcworkspace version

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:30.386
of your project which will
include all the pods for you.

00:13:30.386 --> 00:13:31.760
And then in your
view controller,

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:33.600
if you're using swift,
all you need to do

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:37.860
is add a Firebase import
and you're ready to go.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.870
So now we're going to
go into a live demo.

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:42.070
What are we going to build?

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:45.040
We are going to build a
simple real-time chat app.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:47.390
And it's going to look
something like this.

00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:49.510
Similar to the
video I just played.

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:52.720
It'll be a basic UI table
view, which is just a list.

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:54.450
When you click on
the plus sign, you'll

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:56.530
be able to add a new
message and the messages

00:13:56.530 --> 00:14:00.159
will be displayed
in the table view.

00:14:00.159 --> 00:14:01.950
We're going to use
anonymous authentication

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:05.020
in the application so that I can
show you a little bit about how

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:06.850
Firebase authentication works.

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:09.511
Why would you want to use
anonymous auth in an app?

00:14:09.511 --> 00:14:11.010
Let's say you have
a situation where

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:13.050
you don't want to
require users to log in,

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:15.840
but you want to have
some form of auth.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:17.580
So you want to have
guest accounts.

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:19.787
Anonymous auth would
be great for that.

00:14:19.787 --> 00:14:21.870
And also, with anonymous
auth you still get access

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:23.310
to Firebase's security rules.

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:25.290
So if you want to
enforce security rules

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:27.170
without requiring
user credentials,

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:31.074
anonymous auth is
a great way to go.

00:14:31.074 --> 00:14:32.490
So I'm going to
quickly show video

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:36.480
of how to install Firebase with
CocoaPods, and then will get up

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:37.662
and running with some code.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:43.120
So we're running pod in it.

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:45.540
This will create
a pod file for us.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:49.870
So now I'm opening the pod
file, uncommenting those lines

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:53.930
and adding the Firebase pod
with the correct version.

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:57.130
And I'm going to save
and close the pod file.

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:00.670
And then run pod install.

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:03.050
I'm installing Firebase.

00:15:03.050 --> 00:15:06.120
And now I can see the
xcworkspace version

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:09.600
of my project, which
I'm going to open.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:10.320
And that is it.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:13.580
That is all you have to do
to install the Firebase pod.

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:16.680
So now if we could
go to the demo.

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:17.180
Cool.

00:15:17.180 --> 00:15:19.555
So I've already got a version
of this application running

00:15:19.555 --> 00:15:21.260
that doesn't use Firebase.

00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:23.770
Let's run it in the simulator
and see how it goes.

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:29.730
There you go.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:31.400
So I'm going to
add a chat message.

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:34.200
I'm going to say, hello.

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:35.875
You can see it appears there.

00:15:35.875 --> 00:15:39.820
I'm going to say, hello again.

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:44.720
It's there, but if I quit
the app and reopen it

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:46.530
my message is gone,
which is kind of sad,

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:47.960
it was only saved locally.

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:50.597
We want our chat to be
persisted somewhere which

00:15:50.597 --> 00:15:52.680
is why we want to add
Firebase to the application.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.570
So let's see how
we would do that.

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:56.550
So as you saw in the
video all I need to do

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:58.240
is add a Firebase import.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.590
I've already installed
Firebase pod.

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:04.720
And then the next step is to set
up my Firebase reference, which

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:06.360
is going to go to this URL.

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:13.040
And let's actually take a look
at that URL in the browser.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:15.040
So this is what my Firebase
database looks like.

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:16.380
Let me make that a
little bit bigger.

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:18.296
This is where all the
data from my application

00:16:18.296 --> 00:16:19.906
is going to be stored.

00:16:19.906 --> 00:16:21.530
I haven't added any
security rules yet,

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:22.930
but I'll do that shortly.

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:24.940
And in the log in
and off tab here

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:27.050
you can see that if I
go to anonymous auth

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:30.050
I have it enabled, which means
I have access to that function

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:32.640
to authenticate
users anonymously.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:35.800
Let's go back to the data
and to the application.

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:37.280
And the first
thing we want to do

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:40.390
is, instead of adding the
messages to our local messages

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:42.981
array right when the alert
controller is cleared,

00:16:42.981 --> 00:16:44.980
we're going to add that
in a different function.

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:46.313
So I'm going to get rid of this.

00:16:46.313 --> 00:16:49.340
We're not going to reload
the table view there anymore.

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:52.510
We are going to instead
do that in this push

00:16:52.510 --> 00:16:53.520
to Firebase function.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:55.720
So what we have here is
a local messages array

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:58.940
and that's what we're using
to populate our table view.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:16:59.910
So if we go down here.

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:01.300
Before we write that
push to Firebase

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:02.410
function the first
thing we want to do

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:03.637
is authenticate our users.

00:17:03.637 --> 00:17:05.470
And we're going to do
that in view did load.

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:07.810
This is called only once in
our application lifecycle.

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:10.339
So I'm going to say ref
dot auth anonymously

00:17:10.339 --> 00:17:12.650
with completion block.

00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:15.680
And what this is going to do is
it's going to return an error

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:18.079
and and auth data object.

00:17:18.079 --> 00:17:21.770
And here we're going to say
if error is not equal to null,

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:23.960
so if there's an
error let's just

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:26.480
print the error to the console.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:29.130
Otherwise, let's
print auth data.

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:31.380
Obviously we want to do
something else with auth data,

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:33.300
but I'm just going to show
you what that auth data

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:34.400
object looks like first.

00:17:37.039 --> 00:17:38.830
So here we can see
we've got this auth data

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:42.460
object with our unique
ID for that session.

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:44.650
So instead of printing
it, I'm going to say,

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:49.190
self dot UID equals
auth data dot UID.

00:17:49.190 --> 00:17:51.260
And this self dot
UID is a variable

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.210
I have defined up here.

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:54.980
So now that we've got
users authenticated,

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:57.130
the next thing we want to
do is push that message

00:17:57.130 --> 00:17:59.540
to Firebase with the user's UID.

00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:03.430
So I'm going to create
here an object that's

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:06.580
going to have a key of text
and a value of text, which

00:18:06.580 --> 00:18:10.310
is the text that the user has
inputted into that message box.

00:18:10.310 --> 00:18:12.600
And then we're going to
have a key of user which

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:17.226
is going to have a value of
that UID that we set up here.

00:18:17.226 --> 00:18:18.850
And then to write
this data to Firebase

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:20.090
is just one line of code.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:22.810
So I'm going to say ref
dot child by auto ID.

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.040
And I'm using child
by auto ID because I'm

00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:27.230
going to have a lot
of messages being sent

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:28.820
if this app is a smash hit.

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:31.760
And I want them to each
have their own unique ID.

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:33.460
So I'm using child
by auto ID and then

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:36.980
I'm going to say set value
message data, that data object

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:39.160
that I've defined right here.

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:40.370
So let's run this again.

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:48.170
And I think would be cool if
we had Firebase side by side

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:52.190
here with our chat demo.

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:54.845
So I'm going to
say, hello GCP next.

00:18:57.124 --> 00:18:59.290
And we can see that my
message is added to Firebase.

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:03.800
Just one line of code with
my unique anonymous user ID.

00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:07.924
If I add another
message, hello again.

00:19:07.924 --> 00:19:09.840
We can see that it was
also added to Firebase,

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:11.496
which is pretty cool.

00:19:11.496 --> 00:19:13.495
But you'll notice that
nothing updated in our UI

00:19:13.495 --> 00:19:15.860
because we haven't written
a listener to read data back

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:16.880
from Firebase yet.

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:18.590
So that's what we
want to do next.

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:20.410
So going back to the code.

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:22.590
We're going to do this
in view did appear.

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:25.070
And what we're going to
do is every time the value

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:26.900
that this Firebase
is updated we're

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:29.390
going to add the messages
to this local array

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:32.400
and then we're going to update
the messages array up here

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:34.590
to contain the contents
of that local array

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:37.910
so that our table view will have
all the latest message data.

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:41.210
So here I'm going to say
ref dot observe event type.

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:43.785
And here I want to use the
child added event type.

00:19:43.785 --> 00:19:45.410
Instead of value this
will be triggered

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:48.820
any time a new child is added
to my Firebase reference.

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:51.445
And here we're going
to get back a snapshot.

00:19:54.130 --> 00:19:56.859
And first, let's just print
the value of that snapshot

00:19:56.859 --> 00:19:58.650
so that you can all
see what it looks like.

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:00.525
This will be called once
for the initial data

00:20:00.525 --> 00:20:04.530
and then it's called again
any time the data changes.

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:07.770
So let's play that.

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:11.390
And we can see what we got back
here is all the objects which

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:12.890
are currently in Firebase.

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:15.160
So instead of printing it
here what we want to do

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:17.720
is we want to grab
the value at the texts

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:19.850
and add that to our local array.

00:20:19.850 --> 00:20:21.255
So let's write
the code for that.

00:20:21.255 --> 00:20:22.880
So we're going to
say, let message text

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:26.740
equal snapshot dot
value dot object for key

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:29.200
as a method we have access to.

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:32.454
And I want to grab the
object at the key of text.

00:20:32.454 --> 00:20:34.370
And I know that's always
going to be a string.

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:36.930
So I'm going to
downcast it to a string

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:39.720
and then I'm going to say
a local array dot insert

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:42.690
message text at index 0.

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:44.430
I'm using an insert
here instead of a pen

00:20:44.430 --> 00:20:47.560
because I want to show the
most recent messages first.

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.800
So I'll insert it at index 0.

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:55.580
And then I want to say self dot
messages equals local array.

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:57.370
So I'm going to set
the global messages

00:20:57.370 --> 00:20:59.470
array to this local
array which I'm using

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:01.460
to populate my UI table view.

00:21:01.460 --> 00:21:05.210
And then I'm going to say self
dot table view dot reload data.

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:07.180
And we reload the
table view data.

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:08.450
So let's see this in action.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:14.430
We're building it.

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:16.719
And here we have
in the simulator

00:21:16.719 --> 00:21:18.760
we rendered all those
messages that we just sent.

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:21.460
So let me again pull up
Firebase side by side.

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:25.430
And let's see what happens
when I add a new message.

00:21:25.430 --> 00:21:26.860
Woohoo!

00:21:26.860 --> 00:21:29.370
And see that my message
was added in real time

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:31.790
and was also added to
Firebase, which is pretty cool.

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:34.870
So we've enabled real-time
sync with just a couple lines

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:35.920
of code.

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:38.210
And just to quickly show you
the cross-platform nature

00:21:38.210 --> 00:21:40.935
of Firebase what I've got
over here is a JSFiddle.

00:21:40.935 --> 00:21:43.060
And so I'm going to write
a super simple JavaScript

00:21:43.060 --> 00:21:45.720
application that's connected
to the same Firebase.

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:47.800
And we'll see it update
in the simulator.

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:49.860
So here I'm going
to use JavaScript.

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:52.550
I'm going to say, bar
ref equals new Firebase,

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:55.226
and I'm going to create a
reference to the same Firebase.

00:21:55.226 --> 00:21:58.790
GCP Next dot
Firebase io dot com.

00:21:58.790 --> 00:22:01.960
And then I'm going
to say ref dot push.

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:07.370
Text is going to be
hello from JavaScript.

00:22:07.370 --> 00:22:09.750
And let's run that.

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:11.770
And we can see
that updated here.

00:22:11.770 --> 00:22:14.787
And it was also
added in Firebase.

00:22:14.787 --> 00:22:16.370
But one thing you'll
notice is that it

00:22:16.370 --> 00:22:18.830
doesn't adhere to the schema
that I was following here

00:22:18.830 --> 00:22:21.902
where it has a text
and a user child key.

00:22:21.902 --> 00:22:24.110
And this is where security
rules would come in handy.

00:22:24.110 --> 00:22:27.260
So with security rules
you can see over here,

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:29.630
I'll make this full screen.

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:31.370
By default my
Firebase is globally

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:32.410
readable and write-able.

00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:33.160
Which is not good.

00:22:33.160 --> 00:22:34.701
That means anyone
can read and anyone

00:22:34.701 --> 00:22:37.230
can write to the database here.

00:22:37.230 --> 00:22:38.760
So I should add
some security rules.

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:40.400
So I'm going to do that now.

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:41.980
So I'm going to use
a dollar variable

00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:45.590
to say that for any child
of the rue to my Firebase

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:48.230
this rule should apply.

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:52.090
So I'm going to say to
write to this node we

00:22:52.090 --> 00:22:53.670
have access to--
new data is going

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:55.710
to be whatever data is added.

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:58.660
New data dot has children.

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:01.210
And they were going to
say that it needs to have

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:05.790
a text and a user key there.

00:23:05.790 --> 00:23:07.860
So if I save the rules.

00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:09.640
Rules are saved.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:11.680
And I go here to
the JSFiddle and try

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:14.410
to add the data will see
that it did not succeed

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:16.820
because of my security rules.

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:24.480
But if instead I come in here
and say user Sara and run this

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:25.490
it was called again.

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:27.570
So now we're adhering
to my security rules.

00:23:27.570 --> 00:23:29.990
One other thing I'd like
to add in my security rules

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:32.790
here is I want to make use
of the authentication which

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:33.290
I've added.

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:37.330
So I want to say that autho
cannot be equal to null.

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:41.670
So you need to be authenticated
to write to this Firebase.

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:44.530
So now if I go back to my
Fiddle and try to run this,

00:23:44.530 --> 00:23:48.840
even though this has a
text and a user child key,

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:51.560
it's not going to succeed
because I'm not authenticated

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:53.689
from the JSFiddle.

00:23:53.689 --> 00:23:55.480
So I just wanted to
show you how easy it is

00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:56.710
to build cross-platform apps.

00:23:56.710 --> 00:23:59.239
You can easily build an
iOS, Android, and web app

00:23:59.239 --> 00:24:01.530
that all do the same thing
as long as they're connected

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:03.760
to the same Firebase database.

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:08.140
And again we did that in
just a couple lines of code.

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:09.690
So if we could go
back to the slides.

00:24:19.124 --> 00:24:20.040
Let's see another app.

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:21.730
So you saw a pretty
simple use case.

00:24:21.730 --> 00:24:25.060
A real-time chat up that
updates a UI table view.

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:28.150
Let's see an app that's a
little bit more complex.

00:24:28.150 --> 00:24:29.900
The next thing that
we're going to look at

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:32.300
is an app I built which is
a dashboard for visualizing

00:24:32.300 --> 00:24:33.342
the cloud vision robot.

00:24:33.342 --> 00:24:35.550
How many of you have been
outside and seen the vision

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:37.500
robot at the conference?

00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:38.180
Cool.

00:24:38.180 --> 00:24:39.555
If you haven't
yet, there's still

00:24:39.555 --> 00:24:42.011
time to check it
out after my talk.

00:24:42.011 --> 00:24:43.760
For those of you that
haven't seen it yet,

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:45.884
you're probably wondering
what is this cloud vision

00:24:45.884 --> 00:24:47.880
robot you're talking about.

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:49.460
It is an image
detection robot which

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:52.630
is powered by the
cloud vision API.

00:24:52.630 --> 00:24:54.410
It looks like this.

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:57.010
It's name is vBot.

00:24:57.010 --> 00:24:59.120
And this is a little
bit about how it works.

00:24:59.120 --> 00:25:01.660
So it's a go pi
go robot that has

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:02.959
a raspberry pi running on it.

00:25:02.959 --> 00:25:05.000
And the raspberry pi is
running some python code.

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:08.630
You can think of the raspberry
pi as the brain of the robot.

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:11.240
And the raspberry pi also
has a camera connected to it.

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:14.090
So the camera takes a picture
of whatever is in front of it.

00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:15.865
And then it sends it
to the cloud vision

00:25:15.865 --> 00:25:18.060
API which is able to
return a bunch of metadata

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:19.260
on what's in the object.

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:21.650
So we're using the vision
API's label and face

00:25:21.650 --> 00:25:24.850
detection features here to
return some JSON on what's

00:25:24.850 --> 00:25:25.492
in the image.

00:25:25.492 --> 00:25:26.950
So if there's faces
in the image it

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:30.392
can tell where they are, if
the person is happy or sad.

00:25:30.392 --> 00:25:31.850
And if there's
objects in the image

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:33.740
it can tell what
those objects are.

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:36.530
So the vision API returns
this JSON response.

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:39.234
And then we write that
JSON data to Firebase.

00:25:39.234 --> 00:25:41.400
And we're using Firebase
to power the web dashboard.

00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:42.840
So if you've seen
the vision bot you'll

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:44.756
see a dashboard that
looks something like this

00:25:44.756 --> 00:25:45.954
on the monitors nearby.

00:25:45.954 --> 00:25:47.370
And it displays
the latest picture

00:25:47.370 --> 00:25:50.440
that was taken with the
bot along with the words

00:25:50.440 --> 00:25:51.970
that it's seeing.

00:25:51.970 --> 00:25:54.880
So you've got a web app
to visualize the robot.

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:59.190
And what we want to
add is an iOS app.

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:01.399
So I've taken the robot to
a couple of events so far.

00:26:01.399 --> 00:26:03.273
And I thought it would
be cool, sometimes I'm

00:26:03.273 --> 00:26:04.860
walking between
different demo booths,

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:07.566
I'm walking between venues and
I want to check up on the bots,

00:26:07.566 --> 00:26:09.940
make sure they're all up and
running like they should be.

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:10.860
So I thought I'd
be cool to build

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:13.240
an iOS app that does something
similar to what the web

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:15.379
app does.

00:26:15.379 --> 00:26:17.170
This is what the app
is going to look like.

00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:20.100
It's going to be a basic
master detail view application.

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:21.950
So you've got a
UI table view that

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:25.170
showing the ID of all
of the active bots.

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:27.460
And then when you
click on one of the IDs

00:26:27.460 --> 00:26:30.910
it'll show the detailed view
of what that bot is looking at.

00:26:30.910 --> 00:26:33.420
So here's a picture I took
at my desk a couple days ago.

00:26:33.420 --> 00:26:36.040
You can see it recognized my
face and that I was smiling.

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:37.955
So it drew a green
box around my face.

00:26:37.955 --> 00:26:39.580
And I was holding up
a bottle of water.

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:41.780
The vision API was
able to identify that

00:26:41.780 --> 00:26:44.780
as a bottle of water.

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:47.316
So what is the data going
to look like in Firebase?

00:26:47.316 --> 00:26:48.690
So at the root of
our Firebase we

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:51.750
have a slash bots node
and then under that we

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:54.200
have all the IDs for our bots.

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:56.020
And whenever one
is updated we want

00:26:56.020 --> 00:26:58.560
to change the color of that
cell and display how long

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:00.200
ago the bot was last active.

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:02.700
So here, if it was active
in the last 60 seconds

00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:05.420
I'm displaying an active now.

00:27:05.420 --> 00:27:06.799
That's what the
table looks like.

00:27:06.799 --> 00:27:09.090
And this is a simplified
version of what the table view

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:10.712
controller code will look like.

00:27:10.712 --> 00:27:12.170
So the first thing
that we're doing

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:14.480
is creating a reference
to our Firebase.

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:16.230
And then we're going
to create a local bot

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:18.275
array of f data snapshot.

00:27:18.275 --> 00:27:21.260
F data snapshot is the
data type of the snapshot

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:23.285
that is returned from Firebase.

00:27:23.285 --> 00:27:24.660
And then what we
want to do is we

00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:27.205
want to sync that data from
Firebase into a local array.

00:27:27.205 --> 00:27:28.580
So this is pretty
similar to what

00:27:28.580 --> 00:27:32.580
I did before but we're listening
on the value event type.

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:34.900
And then we're going to loop
through all the bot IDs.

00:27:34.900 --> 00:27:37.550
So we're going to say, for a
bot in snapshot dot children.

00:27:37.550 --> 00:27:39.008
And then within
this function we're

00:27:39.008 --> 00:27:40.920
going to sync all the
data to a local array.

00:27:40.920 --> 00:27:44.610
And I'll show you it
in Xcode momentarily.

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:47.530
So then what will the data in
our detailed view look like?

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:50.010
So this is all the data
we're storing for each bot.

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:51.496
A bunch of different data.

00:27:51.496 --> 00:27:53.870
And this is all data that's
returned from the vision API.

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:55.620
So if you look at
that slash face node,

00:27:55.620 --> 00:27:59.120
the vision API returns a
bunch of data on the emotions

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:02.180
that it detects in the image,
where specific features are

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:03.319
in your face.

00:28:03.319 --> 00:28:05.110
And then we're storing
all this in Firebase

00:28:05.110 --> 00:28:07.290
along with the basic
C4 encoded image

00:28:07.290 --> 00:28:09.170
string of the latest image.

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:10.920
And then we're also
storing all the labels

00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:12.750
that the vision API
finds in Firebase.

00:28:12.750 --> 00:28:15.830
So we want to loop through
those labels and display them.

00:28:15.830 --> 00:28:18.810
The vision API also
returns a confidence score

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:21.270
of how confident it is that
it found that specific label

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:22.250
in the image.

00:28:22.250 --> 00:28:25.650
So we'll display that
in the UI as well.

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:29.180
And this is what our detail view
controller code will look like.

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:32.960
So we create a reference to,
not to the root of our Firebase,

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:35.140
but to the specific
path for that bot.

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:38.620
So this is going to be slash
bot slash whatever bot ID.

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:41.060
And Firebase references
are inexpensive to create.

00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:43.680
So we can create a
new ref for each bot

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:46.680
when that bot cell is clicked.

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:48.350
And so then on
that bot ref we're

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:51.180
going to listen again
on the value event type.

00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:53.990
And then we're going to
grab all the data that's

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:57.410
being returned from the vision
API and store that in a vision

00:28:57.410 --> 00:28:58.930
data object.

00:28:58.930 --> 00:29:01.670
And then we're going to get the
basic C4 encoded image string.

00:29:01.670 --> 00:29:03.540
We want to decode that
and then display it

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:05.394
in our UI image view.

00:29:05.394 --> 00:29:07.310
And then we're going to
get all of the labels.

00:29:07.310 --> 00:29:09.268
And then we're going to
loop through the labels

00:29:09.268 --> 00:29:13.170
and display those
in the UI as well.

00:29:13.170 --> 00:29:15.640
So just to show you what the
format of the data looks like.

00:29:15.640 --> 00:29:18.340
I've got a few sample responses
from the cloud vision API.

00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:19.840
So this is a picture
I took a couple

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:22.544
months ago of some
beignets in New Orleans.

00:29:22.544 --> 00:29:24.960
And I was pretty impressed
with the vision API's response.

00:29:24.960 --> 00:29:26.850
It was able to identify
it as a beignet,

00:29:26.850 --> 00:29:29.730
even though it's a super close
up photo that may not even

00:29:29.730 --> 00:29:31.240
look like desert.

00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:32.305
But it said beignet.

00:29:32.305 --> 00:29:34.190
It was 92% sure.

00:29:34.190 --> 00:29:37.481
It also said desert
and powdered sugar.

00:29:37.481 --> 00:29:39.480
And then here's an example
of the face detection

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:42.000
response to a picture
I took with a friend.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:43.700
So you can see it
returns the result

00:29:43.700 --> 00:29:45.830
of the emotions it detected.

00:29:45.830 --> 00:29:49.200
We can see that joy
likelihood was very likely.

00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:51.210
It also returns a
landmarks object,

00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:53.980
which includes a bunch of
different features in your face

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:58.550
and xy coordinates of where
those are in the image.

00:29:58.550 --> 00:30:01.140
So now let's jump in to
live demo number two.

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:04.430
If we could switch
to my computer.

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:06.920
So I'm going to
minimize this app

00:30:06.920 --> 00:30:09.980
and I'm going to
pull up-- actually

00:30:09.980 --> 00:30:11.630
I'm going to stop
this from running.

00:30:11.630 --> 00:30:15.139
I'm going to pull the bot app.

00:30:15.139 --> 00:30:16.680
I'm actually going
to have Cathy come

00:30:16.680 --> 00:30:18.100
up here to demonstrate this.

00:30:18.100 --> 00:30:24.689
So Cathy here has a bot and
I'm going to run this app

00:30:24.689 --> 00:30:27.230
and we're going to be able to
see what the bot is looking at.

00:30:27.230 --> 00:30:31.120
So this I believe
is bot number 5574.

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:35.580
And we can see that she's
holding up a flower to it.

00:30:35.580 --> 00:30:37.717
The lights are throwing
off a little bit but, could

00:30:37.717 --> 00:30:38.800
be a Christmas decoration.

00:30:41.720 --> 00:30:42.410
Daisy family.

00:30:42.410 --> 00:30:43.552
There you go.

00:30:43.552 --> 00:30:44.760
So there's the bot in action.

00:30:44.760 --> 00:30:45.650
Thank you, Cathy.

00:30:45.650 --> 00:30:46.980
That was awesome.

00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:48.520
So this is what
the app is doing.

00:30:48.520 --> 00:30:50.410
It's got a list of
all the active bots.

00:30:50.410 --> 00:30:52.440
I can click on
them and then I can

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:54.350
see what they're looking at.

00:30:54.350 --> 00:30:57.186
So that's kind of an overview
of what the app does.

00:30:57.186 --> 00:30:58.810
Now I want to walk
you through the code

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:01.437
so that you can see a little
bit more complex app built

00:31:01.437 --> 00:31:02.020
with Firebase.

00:31:02.020 --> 00:31:06.760
So first, I want to show you
the table view controller.

00:31:06.760 --> 00:31:08.630
So here we set up our
Firebase reference.

00:31:08.630 --> 00:31:11.050
I've got a helper
class to do that.

00:31:11.050 --> 00:31:13.691
And we want to have a
variable with the bot ID

00:31:13.691 --> 00:31:16.190
because we're going to use this
when we segue from the table

00:31:16.190 --> 00:31:18.450
view controller to the
detail view controller.

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:20.680
We want to pass that bot
ID so that we can create

00:31:20.680 --> 00:31:22.130
our new Firebase reference.

00:31:22.130 --> 00:31:26.870
And then we also have our local
bot array of f data snapshots.

00:31:26.870 --> 00:31:28.412
So the first thing
we want to do is

00:31:28.412 --> 00:31:30.620
in view did appear we're
going to attach our Firebase

00:31:30.620 --> 00:31:31.610
listener.

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:33.570
And we're going to say, bot ref.

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:35.907
And Firebase has a
couple querying methods

00:31:35.907 --> 00:31:36.990
which are pretty powerful.

00:31:36.990 --> 00:31:38.990
They let you query
by an arbitrary child

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:40.550
key in your Firebase.

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:42.444
So here I am doing a
query ordered by key.

00:31:42.444 --> 00:31:43.860
And remember all
the keys here are

00:31:43.860 --> 00:31:46.940
going to be that list of bot
IDs which you saw in the slide.

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:50.090
And I only want to
query the box with IDs

00:31:50.090 --> 00:31:52.530
starting between devrel
five and devrel six.

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:55.510
All the bots we brought to this
event have an ID of devrel five

00:31:55.510 --> 00:31:57.550
with some other
numbers after it.

00:31:57.550 --> 00:31:59.610
So I got this query here.

00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:02.360
And I'm listening on
the value event type.

00:32:02.360 --> 00:32:03.940
And the first thing
I do is I clear

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:07.500
that local bot array and I'm
going to add all the new bot

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:08.245
data to it.

00:32:08.245 --> 00:32:10.870
So I'm going to loop through the
bots for bots and snapshot dot

00:32:10.870 --> 00:32:12.100
children.

00:32:12.100 --> 00:32:15.500
I'm going to say let bot snap
equal bot as f data snapshot

00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:18.564
since we know it's going to
be of type f data snapshot.

00:32:18.564 --> 00:32:20.730
And then we're going to
append that to the bot array

00:32:20.730 --> 00:32:22.800
and reload the table view.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:24.570
That's how we set
up our table view.

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:27.680
Here is where we write the
code to configure the cell.

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:29.830
So in this line we're
configuring the cell.

00:32:29.830 --> 00:32:32.180
And then remember the cell
on the left side, the text

00:32:32.180 --> 00:32:34.060
on the left was the bot's ID.

00:32:34.060 --> 00:32:37.620
The text on the right was how
long ago the bot was active.

00:32:37.620 --> 00:32:39.974
So here we've got
the bot time stamp.

00:32:39.974 --> 00:32:41.390
We're grabbing
that from Firebase.

00:32:41.390 --> 00:32:43.355
It's stored in
Firebase as a string.

00:32:43.355 --> 00:32:45.730
So we need to actually calculate
how long ago the bot was

00:32:45.730 --> 00:32:46.690
active.

00:32:46.690 --> 00:32:48.755
So I've got a helper
method to do that.

00:32:48.755 --> 00:32:50.380
And then we want to
configure the cell.

00:32:50.380 --> 00:32:53.240
We'll set the text
label to the bot ID.

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:54.930
And we'll set the
detail text label,

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:57.260
which is the text that
appears on the right

00:32:57.260 --> 00:32:58.900
to the active time stamp.

00:32:58.900 --> 00:33:01.960
And then we want to change the
background color of the cell

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:04.140
depending on how long
ago the bot was active.

00:33:04.140 --> 00:33:07.310
So the display is green
if the bot has been active

00:33:07.310 --> 00:33:09.080
in the last minute.

00:33:09.080 --> 00:33:11.780
And then the next thing
we do is set up our segue.

00:33:11.780 --> 00:33:14.930
So here at did select
row at index path,

00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:17.210
this is called whenever
a user selects one

00:33:17.210 --> 00:33:19.170
of the rows in our table view.

00:33:19.170 --> 00:33:21.700
We want to prepare our segue.

00:33:21.700 --> 00:33:25.916
So we're going to set the ID
to whatever bot ID was selected

00:33:25.916 --> 00:33:27.790
and then we're going to
perform the segue bot

00:33:27.790 --> 00:33:29.100
lists to detail view.

00:33:29.100 --> 00:33:32.180
And right here is
where we do that segue.

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:33.990
So in our destination
view controller

00:33:33.990 --> 00:33:36.790
which is going to be the
bot detail view controller.

00:33:36.790 --> 00:33:38.780
We're setting the name
on that view controller

00:33:38.780 --> 00:33:42.760
to the bot's ID of the
bot that was selected.

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:44.450
And then here's
just the functions

00:33:44.450 --> 00:33:47.770
we used to set up our table
view with our bot array data.

00:33:47.770 --> 00:33:50.870
So once we perform
the segue we're here

00:33:50.870 --> 00:33:53.350
in our bot detail
view controller, which

00:33:53.350 --> 00:33:55.030
is pretty short.

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:56.710
So in here we're just
setting the title,

00:33:56.710 --> 00:34:00.289
which is just the heading on
that view to the bots name.

00:34:00.289 --> 00:34:02.330
And then we're creating
a new Firebase reference.

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:04.890
So this reference is going to
be slash bot slash whatever

00:34:04.890 --> 00:34:06.020
bot ID we're viewing.

00:34:06.020 --> 00:34:08.550
Devrel five in this example.

00:34:08.550 --> 00:34:10.739
And then we're adding
another Firebase listener

00:34:10.739 --> 00:34:13.219
to listen to the data only
for that specific bot.

00:34:13.219 --> 00:34:15.840
So bot detail ref dot
observe event type.

00:34:15.840 --> 00:34:17.234
We're listening
on the value type

00:34:17.234 --> 00:34:19.650
again here because we're not
adding new data to this node,

00:34:19.650 --> 00:34:22.389
we're just resetting the data
every time a new image is

00:34:22.389 --> 00:34:24.320
taken by the bot.

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:27.830
So we're grabbing all the data
returned from the vision API.

00:34:27.830 --> 00:34:29.650
We can think of this
as the bot's world.

00:34:29.650 --> 00:34:32.065
So we store that with the key
of world, and then we basic

00:34:32.065 --> 00:34:34.780
C4 decode the image since we're
storing the basic C4 image

00:34:34.780 --> 00:34:36.681
string in Firebase.

00:34:36.681 --> 00:34:38.139
And then we get
the labels that are

00:34:38.139 --> 00:34:39.826
returned from the vision API.

00:34:39.826 --> 00:34:41.659
And we loop through
them and we display them

00:34:41.659 --> 00:34:44.440
so the description is going
to be whatever the label was

00:34:44.440 --> 00:34:47.190
and then score is going
to be the confidence

00:34:47.190 --> 00:34:49.540
score from the vision API.

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:51.550
And we're going to
display that as a percent.

00:34:51.550 --> 00:34:53.790
We add that to the
label string and then

00:34:53.790 --> 00:34:56.420
we just add some
styling to the UI label.

00:34:56.420 --> 00:34:58.140
So again, I just
want to show you

00:34:58.140 --> 00:35:00.590
an example of an app
built with Firebase

00:35:00.590 --> 00:35:02.910
that's a little more complex
than just real-time chat.

00:35:02.910 --> 00:35:04.860
And we can see that this
is super easy to do.

00:35:04.860 --> 00:35:07.900
I've got a table view controller
and my detail view controller,

00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:11.940
and all the data is displaying
in real time with Firebase.

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:13.500
So if we could go
back to the slides.

00:35:18.970 --> 00:35:20.950
So just to recap, this
is what we covered.

00:35:20.950 --> 00:35:23.160
We started with an
overview of Firebase.

00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:26.660
Then I talked about how to get
started with Firebase on iOS.

00:35:26.660 --> 00:35:29.130
And we did one demo,
a real-time chat.

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:31.570
Showed you how to update
a UI table view with chat

00:35:31.570 --> 00:35:34.000
messages stored in Firebase.

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:36.540
And then we did a second demo
with the help from Cathy,

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:39.760
thank you Cathy, to show
a bot visualizer app

00:35:39.760 --> 00:35:42.890
that I built to visualize all
the active cod vision bots

00:35:42.890 --> 00:35:47.270
and what they're looking
at at that point in time.

00:35:47.270 --> 00:35:49.740
So if you want to learn more
the Firebase documentation

00:35:49.740 --> 00:35:51.250
is a great place to go.

00:35:51.250 --> 00:35:54.020
They've got awesome docs for
every platform, iOS, Android,

00:35:54.020 --> 00:35:57.950
and web, with detailed guides
on how to do all sorts of things

00:35:57.950 --> 00:35:59.240
with Firebase.

00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:01.670
And then if you want to learn
more about the vision API,

00:36:01.670 --> 00:36:04.000
if you go to the Google
Cloud platform GitHub,

00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:05.670
the cloud vision
repo has a bunch

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:09.032
of getting started samples
in all sorts of platforms

00:36:09.032 --> 00:36:10.490
to get started with
the vision API.

00:36:10.490 --> 00:36:14.540
There is an iOS sample
in Swift and Objective C.

00:36:14.540 --> 00:36:16.790
And I encourage you to get
in touch with Google Cloud.

00:36:16.790 --> 00:36:18.410
You can follow us on Twitter.

00:36:18.410 --> 00:36:19.940
We have a slack channel.

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:23.570
And we've got a bunch of samples
for all sorts of cloud products

00:36:23.570 --> 00:36:27.810
on the Google Cloud
platform GitHub.

00:36:27.810 --> 00:36:29.310
And thank you all for coming.

00:36:29.310 --> 00:36:30.296
That's all I've got.

00:36:30.296 --> 00:36:34.997
